docs\WORKFLOW.md:107:### End-to-End Deterministic Autonomy
docs\WORKFLOW.md:111:- Design all website features for deterministic autonomy wherever practical. Prefer explicit server-side rules, registries, templates, state transitions, validation constraints, thresholds, scheduled jobs, and event-triggered processing.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:113:- Frontend behavior must be deterministic, accessible, and consistent with authoritative backend decisions.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:116:- Provide appropriate audit logging, monitoring, and deterministic failure handling.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:120:#### Deterministic Autonomy Review and Validation
docs\WORKFLOW.md:122:- Before a feature design is considered near-final, perform a deterministic autonomy review identifying all decisions, governance areas, lifecycle transitions, failure paths, and operational dependencies.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:124:- Validate autonomous behavior through deterministic tests, including negative cases, failure handling, and recovery. Use the automated browser validation guidance below for browser checks, with validation proportional to the change.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:126:### Migration Authority, Approval Gates, and Operational Evidence
docs\WORKFLOW.md:128:#### Standing migration troubleshooting and PostgreSQL catalog lessons ΓÇö 2026-10-17
docs\WORKFLOW.md:196:#### Launch and availability subscription source checkpoint ΓÇö 2026-10-11
docs\WORKFLOW.md:210:Deterministic autonomy review: fixed server eligibility, validation, consent, atomic duplicate suppression, permanent lifecycle receipts, signature verification, withdrawal, sanitization and idempotent repeat rules; no AI or manual per-submission decision. Foundation announcement/geographic approval, future delivery/verification and separately gated migration/deployment remain administrative controls. Offline model/Chromium results do not establish PostgreSQL catalog/ACL/concurrency semantics or deployed readiness.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:218:#### State Request source checkpoint ΓÇö 2026-10-10
docs\WORKFLOW.md:234:#### Stage 5 current operational policies and source implementation
docs\WORKFLOW.md:259:The issuer exclusively writes `authorization-<uuid>.json` containing the bound request and approval references, UTC issuance/expiry, and predecessor. Execution exclusively writes `claim-<environment>-<eight-digit-sequence>.json` before credentials or engines. All contenders for the same environment/predecessor, including different authorization UUIDs, compete for the same deterministic file; only one can succeed. A stale authorization cannot advance to another slot. Partial claims remain evidence and can be reconciled by their exact-byte hash without being repaired or reused. Successful claim creation is followed by exclusively created numbered run events and `terminal-<run-uuid>.json`; denials use separate unique JSON records. Completion records use `pending-authorization-<uuid>.json` / `completed-authorization-<uuid>.json` and `pending-terminal-<run-uuid>.json` / `completed-terminal-<run-uuid>.json`. Their exact fields are `format=1`, `kind=record_completion`, `record` (payload filename), `sha256` (exact payload bytes), `authorization_id`, `run_id` (null for issuance), and `evidence` (empty for issuance; exact-byte claim and acknowledged event hashes for finalization). Only the completed name qualifies; publication never overwrites another record. No failed or uncertain operation is automatically retried.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:263:##### Stage 5 offline validation and deterministic autonomy
docs\WORKFLOW.md:267:Deterministic autonomy review: environment/request selection, approval/hash binding, UTC expiry, claim competition, denial persistence, audit transitions, transaction/commit classification, no-op checks, and recovery blocking use fixed rules without AI. Independent review, deliberate owner issuance, prerequisite attestation, and exceptional reconciliation remain the approved administrative boundaries. Offline success proves tested source behavior only; live PostgreSQL semantics, operational validation, independent artifact approval, and a later testing-only execution authorization remain separate gates.
docs\WORKFLOW.md:273:**APPROVED DESIGN / NOT IMPLEMENTED:** The owner approved the Checkpoint 4 Candidate C migration-authority contract with three refinements: one approved revision per execution in one transaction; migration credential services ending in `/Migrations`; and deterministic, sanitized audit evidence. The specific architecture and staged sequence are governed by the "PostgreSQL Migration Authority Contract ΓÇö Checkpoint 4" section in `WEBSITE_PLAN.md`.
